  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Greer Holiday (GH), Editor-in-Chief &mdash; Neurotrauma Practice. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>The questions below come up repeatedly in TBI case intake. The answers are general and informational; specific cases require evaluation by a licensed catastrophic injury attorney with neurotrauma experience.</p>

  <h2>1. What is the typical settlement value for a brain injury case?</h2>

  <p>Ranges by severity, drawn from published verdict and settlement data:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mild TBI / concussion:</strong> $50K&ndash;$500K, with cases involving documented post-concussive syndrome and vocational impact at the upper end.</li>
    <li><strong>Moderate TBI:</strong> $500K&ndash;$3M, depending on cognitive deficits, vocational impact, and rehabilitation needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Severe TBI (non-vegetative):</strong> $2M&ndash;$15M, depending on the cognitive profile, attendant care needs, and lost earning capacity.</li>
    <li><strong>Severe TBI with vegetative, minimally conscious, or locked-in state:</strong> $5M&ndash;$25M+, with the largest verdicts exceeding $50M.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are wide ranges because the case-specific factors (liability strength, available insurance, jurisdiction, age, neuropsychological findings) drive substantial variation. See our <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a> for the framework and the <a href="/">calculator</a> for a personalized estimate.</p>

  <h2>2. How long does a brain injury case take to resolve?</h2>

  <p>Typical timeline is 1&ndash;3 years for moderate-to-severe TBI cases that resolve by settlement; longer (3&ndash;5+ years) for cases that go to verdict. Several factors stretch the timeline: the medical situation needs to stabilize enough to construct a credible life-care plan (usually 12&ndash;24 months post-injury, sometimes longer), neuropsychological testing needs to be performed at appropriate intervals, and complex multi-defendant cases require more discovery. Mild TBI cases often resolve faster (6&ndash;18 months) because the medical course is shorter.</p>

  <h2>3. What do attorneys typically charge for TBI cases?</h2>

  <p>Catastrophic injury attorneys almost always work on contingency: a percentage of the recovery, with no fee if there is no recovery. Typical contingency rates are 33% if the case settles before suit is filed, 40% if suit is filed, and sometimes higher (45&ndash;50%) for cases that go through trial and appeal. Costs (expert fees, deposition costs, filing fees) are typically advanced by the firm and deducted from the recovery before the contingency calculation in some states and after in others. The retainer agreement should spell this out clearly. State bars regulate maximum contingency rates in some states.</p>

  <h2>4. What if the at-fault party has minimum or no insurance?</h2>

  <p>This is a frequent and painful scenario. Several recovery paths are possible:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Underinsured motorist (UIM) coverage</strong> on the victim's own auto policy. UIM stacks above the at-fault driver's policy and is often the single largest recovery source in low-limits cases.</li>
    <li><strong>Uninsured motorist (UM) coverage</strong> where the at-fault driver had no insurance.</li>
    <li><strong>Umbrella or excess policies</strong> if any are in force.</li>
    <li><strong>Third-party claims</strong>: against an employer if the at-fault driver was working, against a bar or social host under dram shop laws in some states, against vehicle manufacturers for design or component defects, or against premises owners for non-MVA injuries.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal assets</strong> of the at-fault party. Usually limited and rarely the focus.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A good catastrophic injury attorney maps all available coverage and assets early in the case.</p>

  <h2>5. How are future medical and care costs calculated?</h2>

  <p>A life-care planner (typically a certified life care planner, physiatrist, or rehabilitation specialist) reviews the medical record and projects future needs year by year through expected lifespan. Categories include attendant care, ongoing rehabilitation (cognitive, speech, occupational, physical therapy), medications, equipment, home modifications, transportation, and anticipated hospitalizations. An economist then reduces the year-by-year projection to present value using a defensible discount rate. The life-care plan and economic report are the foundation of the future-damages claim.</p>

  <h2>6. Are brain injury settlements taxed?</h2>

  <p>The general rule under IRC &sect; 104(a)(2) is that compensatory damages on account of physical injury are not subject to federal income tax. TBI cases are clearly physical injury, so the bulk of a TBI settlement is generally non-taxable. Exceptions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Punitive damages are generally taxable.</li>
    <li>Interest awarded on the judgment is taxable.</li>
    <li>Some emotional distress damages not tied to physical injury may be taxable.</li>
    <li>The portion attributable to previously-deducted medical expenses may be taxable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The structure of the settlement matters; consult a tax professional or an attorney experienced in settlement structuring.</p>

  <h2>7. Should I take a lump-sum or a structured settlement?</h2>

  <p>It depends. Structured settlements (periodic payments over time) have advantages: the periodic payments are non-taxable, the structure protects against the risk of the lump sum being spent or invested poorly, and for incapacitated TBI plaintiffs, structures pair well with special needs trusts. Disadvantages: less flexibility, dependence on the annuity provider's credit, and (in some cases) lower effective yield than disciplined self-investment. Most large TBI settlements use some combination of lump sum (for immediate medical needs and attorney fees) plus structures (for long-term care). A settlement planner or qualified financial advisor can model the tradeoffs.</p>

  <h2>8. What does GCS mean and why does it matter for my case?</h2>

  <p>The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) is the universal acute-care severity score for brain injury, scored 3&ndash;15. GCS 13&ndash;15 is mild TBI, GCS 9&ndash;12 is moderate, GCS 3&ndash;8 is severe. The lowest documented GCS in the early acute course is often the single most important documentary fact in a TBI case &mdash; it sets the severity classification, which strongly correlates with prognosis and damages. Where the patient was intubated and sedated early, the recorded GCS may not capture the true severity; the field GCS and pre-intubation hospital GCS need to be located in the chart.</p>

  <h2>9. How does a TBI settlement interact with SSDI and Medicaid?</h2>

  <p>SSDI (Social Security Disability Insurance) is generally not affected by a TBI settlement because SSDI is not means-tested. Medicaid, SSI, and other needs-based benefits are means-tested, and a large settlement can disqualify the recipient unless structured properly. The standard solution is a special needs trust (SNT), which holds the settlement proceeds for the benefit of the disabled person without counting against asset limits. A first-party SNT (also called a Medicaid payback trust or (d)(4)(A) trust) is the right structure for an under-65 disabled person receiving a settlement; the assets are administered for the beneficiary's supplemental needs, and at the beneficiary's death, the state Medicaid agency is repaid for benefits provided. Setting up the SNT correctly is a specialized legal task and should not be left until after the settlement closes.</p>

  <h2>10. Can the case value go up or down as the medical situation evolves?</h2>

  <p>Yes. TBI recovery curves are highly variable, especially through the first 12&ndash;24 months. A patient who initially appears severely impaired but recovers significant function will have a lower case value than projected at the acute stage; a patient who plateaus at a worse functional level than initially expected will have a higher value. This is one of the main reasons severe TBI cases generally do not resolve in the first 12 months &mdash; the medical situation needs to stabilize enough to construct a credible life-care plan.</p>

  <h2>11. What about comparative negligence states?</h2>

  <p>Most states reduce recovery by the plaintiff's share of fault. Modified comparative negligence states bar recovery entirely if the plaintiff is more than 50% (or 51%) at fault. Pure comparative negligence states (California, New York, Florida, several others) allow recovery reduced by the plaintiff's share even at high fault percentages. A few states still apply contributory negligence (Alabama, Maryland, North Carolina, Virginia, DC), which historically barred recovery entirely if the plaintiff had any fault &mdash; though this rule is increasingly limited by exceptions. Comparative fault analysis is fact-intensive and is often a major issue in motor vehicle TBI cases.</p>

  <h2>12. Can prognosis really improve, or is the initial outlook set?</h2>

  <p>Prognosis can change significantly, particularly during the first 12&ndash;24 months for moderate-to-severe TBI and during the first 6&ndash;12 months for mild TBI. Initial estimates at the acute care stage are notoriously unreliable; recovery curves vary widely. This is part of why early settlement of moderate-to-severe TBI cases is often poor practice &mdash; the case is undervalued if the patient is going to do worse than expected, and the family loses the option of a higher recovery if facts develop unfavorably. Conversely, settling too late risks losing leverage if the patient recovers more than expected. Timing the settlement to when the medical situation has stabilized but the litigation pressure is high is part of the attorney's craft.</p>

